  "narrative": "The run now has four large, internally robust, and externally within-dataset validated associations: TP53 loss with much weaker USP7 dependency, KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, and MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency. The next scientific priority is not more validation of those accepted effects, but specificity, context, and broad exploration of previously under-tested outcomes. I therefore defer many small pending refinements, including the EGFR amplification strata and residual accepted-effect subgroup checks, because their existing point estimates are far below the clinically meaningful benchmark or the main effects are already accepted with supporting strata. I closed main broad oncogene nulls H1, H2, and H4 because the central questions have been answered in this dataset. The new hypotheses are designed as falsifiable specificity controls. H77 asks whether TP53 loss also weakens NFE2L2 dependency in the same way KEAP1 loss does; H78 asks whether KEAP1 loss also weakens USP7 dependency in the way TP53 loss does. Large cross-biomarker effects would suggest that the accepted reversal associations are not outcome- or pathway-specific and could reflect broader co-mutational or assay structure, whereas near-null results would strengthen the claim that TP53 loss specifically predicts USP7 dependency and KEAP1 loss specifically predicts NFE2L2 dependency. H79 extends the strong SMARCA4-loss effect to the previously under-tested TMED10 outcome; a large effect would raise concern about broader BAF-related or screen-wide dependency structure, while a null result supports TMED10 specificity and leaves SMARCA2 as the main BAF paralog-associated outcome. H80 tests CDKN2A loss versus MDM2 dependency. CDKN2A loss is a plausible p53-pathway predictor independent of direct TP53 loss, and a clinically meaningful negative raw difference, reflected here as a positive signed estimate under direction -1, would identify a new MDM2 dependency marker. I keep H68 and H76 active as complementary POLR2A specificity controls, H69 and H70 as KRAS-wild-type context checks for the reversal effects, H42 and H47 as marker-E specificity controls, H22 as the existing TMED10 marker control, and H72 as an RAS-pathway exploration of NF1 loss versus RIT1 dependency. All interpretations remain dataset-specific associations, not causal claims or generalizable biological claims.",
